@prose p/setup { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "postmortem-2026-07-09" } }
~ A routine Thursday rollout of release 4.2 to the eu-west shard.

The 4.2 rollout began at 09:14 on a Thursday and reached the eu-west shard
forty minutes later. Nothing in the release notes touched the connection pool,
and the canary had been clean for six hours, so the on-call engineer approved
the promotion without waiting for the extended soak. That decision was entirely
routine: the extended soak had not caught anything in eleven weeks, the change
was a dependency bump and two log lines, and the queue of waiting releases was
long enough that everyone had quietly agreed to stop treating the soak as
mandatory. Nobody wrote that agreement down anywhere, which is the part that
mattered later.

@prose p/complication { status: cited, grounds: [p/setup], source: { kind: doc, ref: "postmortem-2026-07-09" } }
~ Latency alarms fired ninety minutes later, and nobody believed them.

The first p95 alarm arrived at 11:02. It was dismissed as a metrics artifact,
because the canary was still green and the error rate had not moved. Two more
alarms followed over the next twenty minutes and were dismissed for the same
reason, each dismissal a little faster than the one before it. By 11:40 the
support queue had noticed before the graphs did, which is the ordering that
tells you the alarm was working and the interpretation was not. The engineer
who finally looked did so because a customer had described the symptom in
words that did not match any of the theories on offer.

@prose p/turn { status: cited, grounds: [p/complication], source: { kind: doc, ref: "postmortem-2026-07-09" } }
~ The pool wait metric had been visible the whole time, on a dashboard nobody opened.

@prose p/resolution { status: cited, grounds: [p/turn], source: { kind: doc, ref: "postmortem-2026-07-09" } }
~ Rolling back the shard restored p95 within four minutes.

@prose p/coda { status: inferred, grounds: [p/resolution] }
~ The canary was clean because it never carried enough concurrency to saturate anything.

@rel p/complication --sequences--> p/setup
